<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Æthelberht of Kent</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 616</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Ethelbert of Kent, Saint Æthelberht</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Æthelberht was king of Kent from about 589 to 616 and the first English king to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Married to the Frankish Christian princess Bertha, he welcomed Augustine's mission from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a> in 597 and gave it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> as its base. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> names him the third overlord of the southern English, and his law code is the earliest surviving document written in English.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aethelberht-of-kent.jpg" alt="Æthelberht of Kent" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giogo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_Ethelbert_jub%C3%A9_Rochester_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Æthelberht was the son of Eormenric, whom he succeeded as king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> traces the Kentish royal line, known as the Oiscings, back to Hengist. Eormenric's name, with an element common among Frankish nobles, hints at links across the Channel, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> records that Æthelberht's father was king of Kent. One other relative is known, his sister Ricole, mother of Sæberht, king of the East Saxons.</p><p>Kent later shows a system of joint kingship, divided between east and west Kent, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> the chief town of the east and Rochester of the west. Some forged early charters imply that Æthelberht ruled jointly with his son Eadbald, but he held authority throughout the kingdom. The dates of his birth and accession are debated, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> are inconsistent with each other.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> lists Æthelberht third among the kings who held imperium over the kingdoms south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a>, and the Chronicle calls such kings bretwalda. His authority was clearest in Essex, where his nephew Sæberht accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and where Æthelberht, not Sæberht, built and endowed St Paul's in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raedwald-of-east-anglia">Rædwald of East Anglia</a> his hold seems to have been weaker, and a passage of Bede on the point can be read in two ways.</p><p>Kent was rich, with strong trade ties to the Continent. Kentish workshops made glass beakers and jewellery, and goods from Kent turn up in cemeteries across the Channel. It has been suggested that Æthelberht took trade from the aristocracy and made it a royal concern, and the first English coinage since Roman times, gold shillings, probably circulated in his reign. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> is thought likely.</p><p>In 597 nearly forty monks under Augustine landed on the Isle of Thanet. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, Æthelberht distrusted them enough to meet them in the open air, so that they could not use sorcery. He allowed them to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> and to preach. It is not known exactly when he became a Christian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Some time after the mission arrived, perhaps in 602 or 603, Æthelberht issued a law code of ninety sections. It sets fines for offences at every level of society, the amount depending on the victim's rank, and it is the earliest surviving code in any Germanic language. Its only early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is the twelfth-century Textus Roffensis. Alfred the Great later drew on it for his own laws.</p><p>Æthelberht died on 24 February 616. His son Eadbald, who was not a Christian, outraged the church by marrying his stepmother. A revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and the expulsion of missionaries from Kent followed, perhaps in part a reaction against Kentish overlordship. Æthelberht came to be regarded as a saint, and is venerated by Catholic and Eastern Orthodox churches.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Æthelberht of Kent” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alfred the Great (r. 871–899) strengthened royal government: his law book drew on the codes of Ine, <b>Æthelberht of Kent</b> and Offa of Mercia, his burhs created a lasting framework of defence and administration, and he presented himself as king of the Anglo-Saxons, all the English not under…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Æthelberht</b>, king from about 589 to 616, married the Frankish Christian princess Bertha and in 597 received Augustine's mission from Pope Gregory, making Canterbury the seat of the English Church.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kent">Kingdom of Kent</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Your words and promises are fair, but because they are new to us, and of uncertain import, I cannot consent to them so far as to forsake that which I have so long observed with the whole English nation.&#8221;</p><footer>Answer to Augustine of Canterbury, 597 (Bede, Ecclesiastical History, book 1, chapter 25) &#183; tr. A. M. Sellar, 1907 &#183; Bede's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/38326/pg38326-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But because you are come from far as strangers into my kingdom, and, as I conceive, are desirous to impart to us those things which you believe to be true, and most beneficial, we desire not to harm you…&#8221;</p><footer>Answer to Augustine of Canterbury, 597 (Bede, Ecclesiastical History, book 1, chapter 25) &#183; tr. A. M. Sellar, 1907 &#183; Bede's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/38326/pg38326-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
